**Definition** Let  $(x_0, f(x_0))$  be a point on the graph of  $y = f(x)$ . The **derivative** of  $f$  at  $x_0$ , written

$$f'(x_0) \quad \text{or} \quad \frac{df}{dx}(x_0) \quad \text{or} \quad \frac{dy}{dx}(x_0),$$

is the slope of the tangent line to the graph of  $f$  at  $(x_0, f(x_0))$ . Analytically,

$$f'(x_0) = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x_0 + h) - f(x_0)}{h} \quad (4)$$

if this limit exists. When this limit does exist, we say that the function  $f$  is differentiable at  $x_0$  with derivative  $f'(x_0)$ .

