### **2 . 5 DIFFERENTIA BILITY A ND C O NTINUITY**

As we saw in Section 2.3, a function f is differcntiahle at xg if, geometrically speaking. its graph has a tangent line at (.x0, f(.~,~)), or analytically speaking. the limit

$$\lim_{h_n \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x_0) + h_n}{h_n} - f(x_0) \quad (8)$$

exists and is the same for every sequence *{h,,}* which converges to 0. If a function is differentiable at every point x,, in its domain D, we say that the function is **diffe re ntia ble . Only** functions whnsc graphs are "smooth curves" have tangent lines everywhere; in fact. mathematicians commonly use the word "smooth" in place of the word "differentiable."

