A point  $\mathbf{p}$  in the Cartesian plane represents a pair of numbers  $(a, b)$  as follows: draw a vertical line  $\ell_1$  and a horizontal line  $\ell_2$  through the point  $\mathbf{p}$ . The vertical line crosses the  $x_1$ -axis at  $a$ , and the horizontal line crosses the  $x_2$ -axis at  $b$ . We associate the pair  $(a, b)$  with the point  $\mathbf{p}$ . To go the other way — to find the point  $\mathbf{p}$  which represents the pair  $(a, b)$  — find  $a$  on the  $x_1$ -axis, and through it draw the vertical line  $\ell_1$ . Find  $b$  on the  $x_2$ -axis, and through it draw the horizontal line  $\ell_2$ . The intersection of the two lines  $\ell_1$  and  $\ell_2$  is the point  $\mathbf{p}$ , which we will sometimes write as  $\mathbf{p}(a, b)$ . The number  $a$  is called the **x-coordinate** of  $\mathbf{p}$ , and  $b$  is called the **x<sub>2</sub>-coordinate** of  $\mathbf{p}$ . In Figure 10.3 we show a number of points and their coordinates.

