Using these diagrams, one can use geometry to study the effects of changes in parameters or in exogenous variables, just as we did analytically in the exercises in Section 9.3. For example, if G or I' increases or if t,, decreases, then the right hand side of the IS-equation increases and the lS-line shifts outward as in Figure 10.34. The result is an increase in the equilibrium Y and r, just as we found in Exercise 9.15. Note that this result would hold even if the slope of the IS-line were positive, as long as it was less than the slope of the LM-line.

