# **11.2 SPANNING SETS**

Let vI, , vk be a fixed set *ofk vectors in* **R". In** the last section, we spoke of the **set** of all **linear combinations** of ~1, , YX,

$$\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{v}_1, , \mathbf{v}_k] \equiv \{c_1\mathbf{v}_1 + + c_k\mathbf{v}_k : c_1, , c_k \in \mathbf{R}\},$$

and called it the set generated or **spanned** by vI,. , vk.

Suppose that we are given a subset *V* of **R".** It is reasonable to ask whether or not there exists v,, , vk in **R"** such that every vector in *V* can be written as a linear combination of v,, , vk:

$$\mathbf{v} = \mathcal{L}[\mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_k]. \quad (10)$$

When (10) occurs, we say tha t vI, , vq s&m " *V.*

