### 12.3 OPEN SETS

Our discussion of sequences leads naturally to the study of open and closed sets in  $\mathbf{R}^m$ . The definition of a closed set directly requires the concept of a convergent sequence. The definition of an open set requires the use of  $\varepsilon$ -balls. Although these two definitions seem unrelated, we will see that the concepts are truly complementary.

We start with open sets since they are the most basic topological construction. For a vector  $\mathbf{z}$  in  $\mathbf{R}^m$  and a positive number  $\varepsilon$ , the  $\varepsilon$ -ball about  $\mathbf{z}$  is  $B_\varepsilon(\mathbf{z}) = \{\mathbf{x} \in \mathbf{R}^m : \|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z}\| < \varepsilon\}$ . Sometimes  $B_\varepsilon(\mathbf{z})$  is called the open  $\varepsilon$ -ball to distinguish it from the closed ball  $\{\mathbf{x} \in \mathbf{R}^m : \|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z}\| \leq \varepsilon\}$ , which includes the boundary. Open halls are important examples of a more general class of sets called open sets.

**Definition** A set  $S$  in  $\mathbf{R}^m$  is open if for each  $\mathbf{x} \in S$ , there exists an open  $\varepsilon$ -ball around  $\mathbf{x}$  completely contained in  $S$ :

$$\mathbf{x} \in S \implies \text{there is an } \varepsilon > 0 \text{ such that } B_\varepsilon(\mathbf{x}) \subset S.$$

The word “open” has a connotation (of “no boundary”: from any point one can always move a little distance in *any* direction and still he in the set). The definition

<!-- page 240 -->

of an open set makes this idea precise: each element in an open set contains a whole ball around it that lies in the set. Consequently, open sets cannot contain their “boundary points.”

*Example 12.4* The interval

$$(0, 1) \equiv \{x \in \mathbb{R} : 0 < x < 1\}$$

is an open set. If  $b$  is a point in  $(0, 1)$ , then  $b \neq 0$  or  $1$ . The number  $h/2$  is closer to 0 than  $b$  and is still in  $(0, 1)$ , while the number  $b + (1 - b)/2$  is closer to 1 than  $b$  and is still in  $(0, 1)$ . If  $\varepsilon = \min\{b/2, (1 - b)/2\}$ , the interval  $(b - \varepsilon, b + \varepsilon)$  is an open interval about  $b$  in  $(0, 1)$ . (Check.)

The definition of an open set also implies that such sets are “thick” or “full-dimensional,” since an open set in  $\mathbb{R}^m$  contains an  $m$ -dimensional ball around each of its points. Consequently, a line in  $\mathbb{R}^2$  is not an open set. As indicated in Figure 12.4, because a line is a one-dimensional subset of  $\mathbb{R}^2$ , the ball around any point on the line contains points which are not on the line. Similarly, a line or plane in  $\mathbb{R}^3$  cannot be open and one-point sets are never open.

![](pg_171__page_70_Figure_129.jpeg)

> **Image description:** Figure 12.4 illustrates why a line in R^2 is not an open set. It shows a straight line with a negative slope intersecting the Cartesian coordinate axes. A point on the line in the fourth quadrant is encircled by an open ball (a small shaded circular region). The ball extends beyond the line into the surrounding plane, visually demonstrating that any neighborhood around a point on the line contains points in R^2 that do not lie on the line.

A ball about any point on a line in  $\mathbb{R}^2$  contains points not on the line.

