### 12.4 CLOSED SETS

The concept of a closed set also plays a fundamental role in the geometry and analysis of Euclidean spaces.

**Definition** A set  $S$  in  $\mathbb{R}^m$  is **closed** if, whenever  $\{\mathbf{x}_n\}_{n=1}^\infty$  is a convergent sequence completely contained in  $S$ , its limit is also contained in  $S$ .

The definition of a closed set states that if  $\mathbf{x}$  is a given point and if there are points in a closed set  $F$  which are arbitrarily close to  $\mathbf{x}$ , then  $\mathbf{x}$  must be in  $F$  too.

<!-- page 243 -->

Consequently, a closed set must contain all its "boundary points," just the opposite of the situation with open sets. In fact, closedness and openness are complementary properties in that the complement of an open set is closed and the complement of a closed set is open. Recall that the **complement** of a set S is the set of all points that are not in S.

**Theorem 12.9** A set S in **Rm is closed** if and only if its complement, S' = **R'" S,** is open.

