Students usually develop a geometric intuition for concave and convex functions of one variable in Calculus I. They can recognize a concave function by its graph because, as Figure 21.2 illustrates, the inequality (1) in the definition of a concave function has the following geometric interpretation:

A function  $f$  of  $n$  variables is concave if and only if any secant line connecting two points on the graph of  $f$  lies *below* the graph. A function is convex if and only if any secant line connecting two points on its graph lies *above* its graph.

<!-- page 481 -->

![](pg_472__page_10_Figure_11.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A 2D coordinate graph illustrating the geometric definition of a concave function. The x-axis shows three labeled points: $y$, a linear combination $tx + (1-t)y$ between $y$ and $x$, and $x$. A concave curve opens downward, passing through points $(y, f(y))$ and $(x, f(x))$, which are marked with solid dots and connected by a straight secant line segment. Vertical dashed lines extend up to the curve and the secant line from $tx + (1-t)y$. On the y-axis, the height of the curve at this point is labeled $f(tx + (1-t)y)$, which lies strictly above the height of the secant line at the same x-value, labeled $tf(x) + (1-t)f(y)$. Below these is $f(y)$ on the y-axis, corresponding to the point at $y$.

*The geometric interpretation of the definition of a concave function.*

**Figure  
21.2**

This property is illustrated in Figures 21.3 and 21.4, which present the graphs of two prototypical convex functions:  $y = x^2$  and  $z = x_1^2 + x_2^2$ .

In developing an intuition for concave functions of several variables and in proving theorems about their properties, it is useful to notice that a function of  $n$  variables defined on a convex set  $U$  is concave if and only if its restriction to any line segment in  $U$  is a concave function of *one* variable. This should be intuitively clear since the definition (1) of a concave function is a statement about its behavior on line segments. Because it is such a useful fact, we provide a careful analytical proof. In the remainder of this section, we will use this result to reduce the proofs of theorems about concave functions on  $\mathbf{R}^n$  to statements about concave functions of a single variable.

