23.34 Consider a stock market with two stocks A and B. Stock A always sells for \$5 or \$10; stock B always sells for \$6 or \$12. If stock A is selling for \$5 today, there is a 75 percent chance it will sell for \$5 tomorrow. If stock A is selling for \$10 today, there is a 90 percent chance that it will sell for \$10 tomorrow. If stock B is selling for \$6 today, there is a 90 percent chance that it will sell for \$6 tomorrow. If stock B is selling for \$12 today, there is a 70 percent chance it will sell for \$12 tomorrow. Compute and compare the average costs of stocks A and B. The hypothesis that stock market prices follow a Markov process is one form of the **efficient market hypothesis**.

