**24.6** Show directly that formula (10) yields a solution to the linear differential equation  $\dot{y} = a(t)y + b(t)$ .

**24.7** Case 4 for linear differential equations,  $\dot{y} = a(t)y + b(t)$ , is the generalization of the previous three cases.

1. Show that expression (10) contains the general solutions of the previous three cases.
2. Use Case 4's integrating factor method to derive the solutions of the first three cases.

