25.11**

(1, 3). If the population were on one side of this separatrix and some exogenous shock pushed it to the other side, the long-run behavior of the ecology would be very different. This example illustrates some of the fragility that is inherent in ecological systems.

One can easily see in Figure 25.12 the geometric realization of the stabilities that we calculated analytically for the four equilibria. Equilibria (4, 0) and (0, 6) are asymptotically stable, but not globally so. Equilibria (0, 0) and (1, 3) are unstable; small perturbations from either of these equilibria will move the ecological system toward one of the two stable equilibria.

System (32) is a special case of the general competing species system (1). There are actually four different cases for the general system (1), depending on how the  $\dot{x} = 0$  and  $\dot{y} = 0$  isoclines intersect in the positive quadrant. Exercise 25.20 asks you to work out examples of the three remaining cases.

