<!-- page 827 -->

# Trigonometric Functions

Most of the specific functions encountered in elementary mathematical approaches to economics are polynomials, quotients of polynomials (rational functions), or exponentials. As the independent variable  $x$  goes to infinity, the graph of each of these three types of functions either goes to infinity (very quickly for exponential functions) or approaches a finite horizontal asymptote. None of these functional forms can model the regular periodic patterns that play an important role in the social, biological, and physical sciences: business cycles and agricultural seasons, heart rhythms, and hormone level fluctuations, and tides and planetary motions. The basic functions for studying regular periodic behavior are the *trigonometric functions*, especially the sine, cosine, and tangent functions.

## A2.1 DEFINITIONS OF THE TRIG FUNCTIONS

The domain of the trigonometric functions is more naturally the set of all geometric angles than some set of real numbers. We start with that approach and measure angles in *degrees* for the time being.

![](pg_771__page_57_Picture_13.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A geometric diagram showing an angle labeled with the Greek letter theta (θ) at vertex A. The angle is formed by two rays meeting at point A: an initial ray labeled l_1 and a terminal ray labeled l_2. An arrow indicates the counterclockwise rotation from ray l_1 to ray l_2. The caption below states: "Angle θ is formed by rotating ray l_1 to l_2."

**Figure  
A2.1**

<!-- page 828 -->

Consider the acute angle  $\theta$  in Figure A2.1 formed by the rays  $\ell_1$  and  $\ell_2$  from the point A. Think of  $\theta$  as being swept out by a *counterclockwise* rotation about the point A from ray  $\ell_1$  to ray  $\ell_2$ . In this case, we will call  $\ell_1$  the initial ray and  $\ell_2$  the terminal ray of angle  $\theta$ . Pick a point B on the terminal ray  $\ell_2$  and draw a perpendicular line  $n$  from B to the initial ray  $\ell_1$ , intersecting  $\ell_1$  at the point C as in Figure A2.2. Triangle  $ABC$  is a right triangle with *hypotenuse AB*, *opposite leg BC*, and *adjacent leg AC*, relative to the angle  $\theta$ .

![](pg_771__page_58_Picture_15.jpeg)

> **Image description:** Figure A2.2 illustrates an acute angle θ with vertex A, formed by an initial ray ℓ1 along the bottom and a terminal ray ℓ2 extending upward to the right. A point B is located on ray ℓ2. A line segment n is drawn from point B perpendicular to ray ℓ1, intersecting ray ℓ1 at point C, indicated by a right-angle symbol at C. This forms a right triangle ABC with angle θ at vertex A.

**Figure A2.2**

*Computing the sine, cosine, and tangent of  $\theta$ .*

The six trigonometric functions are defined by taking ratios of the lengths of these three sides of triangle  $ABC$ :

$$\text{sine } \theta = \frac{\text{opposite leg}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{\|BC\|}{\|AB\|}.$$

$$\text{cosine } \theta = \frac{\text{adjacent leg}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{\|AC\|}{\|AB\|}.$$

$$\text{tangent } \theta = \frac{\text{opposite leg}}{\text{adjacent leg}} = \frac{\|BC\|}{\|AC\|}.$$

$$\text{cotangent } \theta = \frac{\text{adjacent leg}}{\text{opposite leg}} = \frac{\|AC\|}{\|BC\|}.$$

$$\text{secant } \theta = \frac{\text{hypotenuse}}{\text{adjacent side}} = \frac{\|AB\|}{\|AC\|}.$$

$$\text{cosecant } \theta = \frac{\text{hypotenuse}}{\text{opposite leg}} = \frac{\|AB\|}{\|BC\|}.$$

<!-- page 829 -->

where  $\|AC\|$  denotes the *length* of line segment  $AC$ . These six functions represent all the possible ratios of the three sides of triangle  $ABC$ . The first three trigonometric functions — sine, cosine, and tangent — are the most important of the six. As the following theorem asserts, the last four can be expressed in terms of the first two.

**Theorem A2.1** Every trig function can be expressed as a quotient of sine and cosine. In particular,

$$\begin{aligned} \tangent \theta &= \frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta}, & \cotangent \theta &= \frac{\cos \theta}{\sin \theta}, \\ \secant \theta &= \frac{1}{\cos \theta}, & \text{and} & \cotsecant \theta &= \frac{1}{\sin \theta}. \end{aligned}$$

The above definitions are independent of how far the point  $B$  is from  $A$  on the second ray  $\ell_2$ . To see this, suppose that  $B'$  is another point on  $\ell_2$ . Let  $n'$  be the perpendicular line from  $B'$  to  $\ell_1$ , meeting  $\ell_1$  at the point  $C'$ , as indicated in Figure 2.3. Since  $n$  and  $n'$  are both perpendicular to  $\ell_1$ , they are parallel to each other. Therefore, the corresponding sides of triangles  $ABC$  and  $AB'C'$  are parallel to each other. By a fundamental result of the Euclidean geometry one studies in high school, triangles  $ABC$  and  $AB'C'$  are similar to each other; they have equal sets of angles, and therefore the ratios of their corresponding sides are equal. Therefore, the six trig functions have the same values whether one uses triangle  $ABC$  or triangle  $AB'C'$ .

![](pg_771__page_59_Picture_16.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A geometric diagram showing two rays, l1 and l2, originating from vertex A and forming an angle theta (θ). Ray l1 lies below ray l2. On ray l2, two distinct points are marked: B and B' (further from A). From point B, a line segment n drops perpendicularly to ray l1, intersecting it at point C and forming right-angled triangle ABC. From point B', a parallel line segment n' drops perpendicularly to ray l1, intersecting it at point C' and forming a larger right-angled triangle AB'C'. Right-angle symbols are marked at vertices C and C'.

**Figure  
A2.3**

<!-- page 830 -->

For angles that do not lie between  $0^\circ$  and  $90^\circ$ , there is a sign convention necessary for a consistent definition of the trigonometric functions. The simplest way to describe this convention is to choose the point  $B$  in Figure A2.2 so that it is always  $1$  unit away from the tip of the angle at  $A$ . Since the orientation of the angle under study should not make a difference, we can, without any loss of generality, always take the point  $A$  at the tip of the angle to be the origin of some  $xy$ -coordinate system, and its initial ray  $\ell_1$  to be the positive  $x$ -axis. To carry out this approach, draw the circle  $S$  of radius  $1$ , the *unit circle*. For any angle  $\theta$ , think of  $\theta$  as being swept out counterclockwise by rays from the origin starting with the positive  $x$ -axis. As before, call the terminal ray  $\ell_2$ . Choose the point  $B$  on  $\ell_2$   $1$  unit from the origin, that is, on the unit circle  $S$ , as indicated in Figure A2.4.

![](pg_771__page_60_Picture_12.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A geometric diagram depicting the unit circle centered at the origin A in a Cartesian coordinate system. An angle theta is formed between the positive x-axis (labeled line l1) and a terminal ray (labeled line l2) extending into the first quadrant. A point B lies on the unit circle along line l2, at a distance of 1 unit from the origin A. A line segment drops vertically from B to point C on the positive x-axis, forming a right triangle ABC with the right-angle mark at C. The horizontal distance from A to C is labeled as b1, and the vertical distance from C to B is labeled as b2.

**Figure A2.4** Measuring  $\theta$  as a counterclockwise rotation on the unit circle  $S$  from the positive  $x$ -axis.

Suppose that the coordinates of the point  $B$  are  $(b_1, b_2)$  in this coordinate system. Then, the perpendicular segment from  $B$  to the  $x$ -axis  $\ell_1$  meets  $\ell_1$  at the point  $C = (b_1, 0)$ ; furthermore,

$$\|AC\| = b_1 \quad \text{and} \quad \|BC\| = b_2.$$

Since  $\|AB\| = 1$  in this approach, the above definitions of the trigonometric functions become

$$\begin{aligned} \sin \theta &= \frac{b_2}{1} = b_2, & \tan \theta &= \frac{b_2}{b_1}, & \sec \theta &= \frac{1}{b_1}, \\ \cos \theta &= \frac{b_1}{1} = b_1, & \cot \theta &= \frac{b_1}{b_2}, & \csc \theta &= \frac{1}{b_2}. \end{aligned} \tag{1}$$

<!-- page 831 -->

We now extend formally the definitions of these functions to all angles. For any angle  $\phi$ , sweep out  $\phi$  counterclockwise on the unit circle beginning with the positive  $x$ -axis, and let  $B(b_1, b_2)$  be the point where the terminal ray intersects the unit circle  $S$ . Then, the six trigonometric functions are defined by the six expressions in (1);  $b_1$  and  $b_2$  can be positive or negative depending on the size of  $\phi$ . For example, since  $b_2$  is negative when  $B$  lies in the third quadrant (where  $x_1$  and  $x_2$  are negative) and in the fourth quadrant (where  $x_1$  is positive and  $x_2$  is negative),  $\sin \phi$  is negative when  $\phi$  lies between  $180^\circ$  and  $360^\circ$ . Similarly, the tangent and cotangent functions are negative precisely when  $B$  lies in the second or fourth quadrants, that is, when  $90^\circ < \phi < 180^\circ$  or  $270^\circ < \phi < 360^\circ$ .

## A2.2 GRAPHING TRIG FUNCTIONS

If we use the definitions in (1) to graph the sine function, we need only keep track of  $b_2$  as  $\theta$  goes from  $0^\circ$  to  $360^\circ$ . At  $\theta = 0^\circ$ ,  $b_2 = 0$  and  $\sin 0 = 0$ . As  $\theta$  rises from  $0^\circ$  to  $90^\circ$ ,  $b_2$  rises from 0 to 1. As  $\theta$  passes from  $90^\circ$  to  $180^\circ$  to  $270^\circ$ ,  $b_2$  decreases from +1 to 0 to -1. As  $\theta$  goes from  $270^\circ$  to  $360^\circ$ ,  $b_2$  increases from -1 back to 0. As  $\theta$  increases beyond  $360^\circ$ ,  $B$  moves once again around the unit circle. If we move  $B$  clockwise around the unit circle  $S$ , we sweep out *negative angles* (by definition), but we continue to use the formulas in (1) to define their sine, cosine, etc. We conclude that the graph of  $\theta \mapsto \sin \theta$  is the curve in Figure A2.5.

![](pg_771__page_61_Figure_12.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A line plot depicting the graph of the sine function, y = sin(theta), showing two complete periods ranging from -180 degrees to 540 degrees on the horizontal axis and -1 to +1 on the vertical axis. The curve passes through (0, 0), reaches a maximum of +1 at 90 degrees, intersects the x-axis at 180 degrees, reaches a minimum of -1 at 270 degrees, and completes one full cycle at 360 degrees, continuing the same sinusoidal pattern into both positive and negative directions.

The graph of  $\theta \mapsto \sin \theta$ .

Figure  
A2.5

Similarly, by keeping track of  $b_1$  as  $B$  moves around the unit circle, we generate the graph of the cosine function pictured in Figure A2.6. By keeping track of  $b_2/b_1$  as  $B$  moves around the unit circle, we generate the graph of the tangent function in Figure A2.7. Note that as  $\theta$  moves toward  $90^\circ$ ,  $B$  approaches  $(0, 1)$  and  $\tan \theta = b_2/b_1$  approaches  $+\infty$ .

Why do we need more than one function to keep track of angles? For one thing, as Figures A2.5 and A2.6 indicate, for every number  $y$  between  $-1$  and  $+1$ , there are two angles  $\theta_1$  and  $\theta_2$  between  $0^\circ$  and  $360^\circ$  such that  $\sin \theta_1 = \sin \theta_2$ . However, for these two angles,  $\cos \theta_1$  and  $\cos \theta_2$  have different signs. For example,  $\sin 180^\circ = \sin 0^\circ = 0$ , but  $\cos 180^\circ = -1$  and  $\cos 0^\circ = +1$ . Since  $(b_1, b_2) = (\cos \theta, \sin \theta)$ , cosine and sine together completely specify each angle.

<!-- page 832 -->

![](pg_771__page_62_Figure_12.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A plot showing the cosine curve, $y = \cos(\theta)$, with the horizontal axis representing angle $\theta$ in degrees and the vertical axis representing values from $-1$ to $+1$. The graph displays a continuous sinusoidal wave passing through key points: a peak at $(0^\circ, 1)$, a zero crossing at $(90^\circ, 0)$, a trough at $(180^\circ, -1)$, a zero crossing at $(270^\circ, 0)$, another peak at $(360^\circ, 1)$, and another zero crossing at $(450^\circ, 0)$. To the left, it extends to a zero crossing at $(-90^\circ, 0)$.

**Figure  
A2.6**

*The graph of  $\theta \mapsto \cos \theta$ .*

![](pg_771__page_62_Figure_15.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A plot of the tangent function y = tan(x) shown over multiple periods. The graph consists of repeating, S-shaped curves passing through the x-axis and increasing vertically between dashed vertical asymptotes.

**Figure  
A2.7**

*The graph of  $\theta \mapsto \tan \theta$ .*

In addition, each trig function has its own advantages and its own uses. For example, the sine and tangent functions are natural since as  $\theta$  moves from  $-90^\circ$  to  $0^\circ$  to  $+90^\circ$ , the sine shadows it while moving from  $-1$  to  $0$  to  $+1$  and the tangent shadows it while moving from  $-\infty$  to  $0$  to  $+\infty$ . The tangent function is especially useful in applications because its definition involves the length of the two legs of the defining right triangle and, in fact, exactly tracks the *slope* of the hypotenuse

<!-- page 833 -->

of that triangle. On the other hand, as Figure A2.6 indicates, the cosine function satisfies

$$\cos \theta = \cos(-\theta); \quad (2)$$

that is, the cosine is an *even function*. In particular, the cosine of an angle is independent of whether the angle is measured clockwise or counterclockwise.

### A2.3 THE PYTHAGOREAN THEOREM

For the rest of this section we will use some of the basic Euclidean geometry that one studies in high school to derive useful properties of the trigonometric functions. The key geometric principle is the **Pythagorean Theorem**: the sum of the squares of the legs of a right triangle equals the square of the length of the hypotenuse;  $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$  in Figure A2.8.

![](pg_771__page_63_Picture_21.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A right triangle labeled with standard side notation. The horizontal leg at the bottom is labeled 'a', the vertical leg on the right is labeled 'b', and the hypotenuse is labeled 'c'. A right-angle square marker is present at the bottom-right vertex between sides 'a' and 'b'.

By the Pythagorean Theorem,  $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ .

**Figure  
A2.8**

The application of the Pythagorean Theorem to Figure A2.4 yields

$$b_1^2 + b_2^2 = 1, \quad (3)$$

since the hypotenuse has length 1. Using the definitions (1) of the trig functions, equation (3) translates to

$$\cos^2 \theta + \sin^2 \theta = 1 \quad \text{for all angles } \theta. \quad (4)$$

Dividing (3) through by  $b_1^2$  yields

$$1 + \left( \frac{b_2}{b_1} \right)^2 = \left( \frac{1}{b_1} \right)^2 \quad \text{or} \quad 1 + \tan^2 \theta = \sec^2 \theta. \quad (5)$$

<!-- page 834 -->

$$\left(\frac{b_1}{b_2}\right)^2 + 1 = \left(\frac{1}{b_2}\right)^2 \quad \text{or} \quad \cot^2 \theta + 1 = \csc^2 \theta. \quad (6)$$

#### A2.4 EVALUATING TRIGONOMETRIC FUNCTIONS

To understand the trigonometric functions a little better, we evaluate the sine, cosine, and tangent functions at the three important acute angles:  $30^\circ$ ,  $45^\circ$ , and  $60^\circ$ . These evaluations rely on the basic result of Euclidean geometry that two sides of a triangle are equal if and only if the angles opposite these sides are equal.

For the  $45^\circ$  angle in Figure A2.9, we have drawn right triangle  $OBC$ , motivated by Figure A2.2. (We have put aside the unit circle approach for a moment.) Since  $\angle O = 45^\circ$  and  $\angle OCB = 90^\circ$ ,  $\angle OBC = 45^\circ$ . (The angles of a triangle sum to  $180^\circ$ .) Since  $\angle COB = \angle OBC$ ,  $\|OC\| = \|CB\|$ . If we chose  $B$  so that  $\|OC\| = \|CB\| = 1$ , then by the Pythagorean Theorem,

$$\|OB\|^2 = \|OC\|^2 + \|BC\|^2 = 1 + 1 = 2.$$

So,  $\|OB\| = \sqrt{2}$ . We conclude that

$$\begin{aligned} \sin 45^\circ &= \frac{\|BC\|}{\|OB\|} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 0.7071, \\ \cos 45^\circ &= \frac{\|OC\|}{\|OB\|} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 0.7071, \\ \tan 45^\circ &= \frac{\|BC\|}{\|OC\|} = \frac{1}{1} = 1. \end{aligned} \quad (7)$$

![](pg_771__page_64_Picture_24.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A right-angled triangle OBC set within an angle ray system. Vertex O is located at the origin at the bottom left, with an acute angle of 45 degrees between the horizontal line OC and the inclined ray OB. The right angle is located at vertex C on the horizontal line, indicated by a square angle symbol. The segment BC is perpendicular to OC and has a tick mark indicating a length marker. The angle at vertex B, inside the triangle OBC between line segment OB and vertical line segment BC, is also labeled as 45 degrees.

**Figure  
A2.9**

*The 45-45-90 isosceles triangle.*

<!-- page 835 -->

To compute the values of the trig functions at  $30^\circ$  and  $60^\circ$ , consider the 60-60-60 equilateral triangle  $ABC$  in Figure A2.10, and draw the bisector of  $\angle B, BD$ . Since

$$\angle A = \angle C = 60^\circ \quad \text{and} \quad \angle ABD = \angle DBC = 30^\circ, \quad (8)$$

 $\angle ADB = \angle CDB = 90^\circ$ . We use the right triangle  $ABD$  to evaluate the trig functions at  $30^\circ$  and at  $60^\circ$ .

![](pg_771__page_65_Picture_18.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A geometric diagram of an equilateral triangle ABC with base AC and top vertex B. An altitude BD is drawn from vertex B perpendicular to base AC at point D, forming right angles at D (indicated by right-angle symbols). The interior angles at vertices A and C are both labeled as 60 degrees.

*The 60-60-60 equilateral triangle.*

**Figure  
A2.10**

Recall from high school geometry that two triangles are congruent if in the two triangles, two corresponding pairs of angles and the sides between them are equal (angle-side-angle rule). Therefore, (8) implies that triangles  $ABD$  and  $BDC$  are congruent; and so,

$$\|AD\| = \|DC\| = \frac{1}{2}\|AB\|.$$

If we choose units so that  $\|AD\| = 1$ , then  $\|AB\| = 2$ , and by the Pythagorean Theorem,

$$\|BD\| = \sqrt{\|AB\|^2 - \|AD\|^2} = \sqrt{3}.$$

It follows that

$$\sin 60^\circ = \frac{\|BD\|}{\|AB\|} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \approx 0.866,$$

$$\cos 60^\circ = \frac{\|AD\|}{\|AB\|} = \frac{1}{2} = 0.5,$$

<!-- page 836 -->

$$\tan 60^\circ = \frac{\|BD\|}{\|AD\|} = \sqrt{3} \approx 1.732,$$

$$\sin 30^\circ = \frac{\|AD\|}{\|AB\|} = \frac{1}{2} = 0.5,$$

$$\cos 30^\circ = \frac{\|BD\|}{\|AB\|} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \approx 0.866,$$

$$\tan 30^\circ = \frac{\|AD\|}{\|BD\|} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \approx 0.577.$$

These values are consistent with the graphs in Figures A2.5, A2.6, and A2.7.

## A2.5 MULTIANGLE FORMULAS

Finally, we consider the behavior of the trigonometric functions with regard to the sums and differences of angles. The following theorem, presented without proof, summarizes these rules.

**Theorem A2.2** For any two angles  $a$  and  $b$ ,

$$\sin(a + b) = \sin a \cos b + \cos a \sin b,$$

$$\sin(a - b) = \sin a \cos b - \cos a \sin b,$$

$$\cos(a + b) = \cos a \cos b - \sin a \sin b,$$

$$\cos(a - b) = \cos a \cos b + \sin a \sin b.$$

In the special case that  $a = b$ ,

$$\sin 2a = 2 \sin a \cos a,$$

$$\cos 2a = \cos^2 a - \sin^2 a.$$

Theorem A2.2 will be important in deriving the formulas of  $(\sin x)^l$  and  $(\cos x)^l$ . Of course, it also extends the set of angles whose sines and cosines can be computed exactly. For example, knowing the sin and cos of  $45^\circ$  and  $30^\circ$ , we can compute the sin and cos of  $15^\circ$  and  $75^\circ$ .

We have formulated the trigonometric functions as functions of geometric angles. We can easily consider each of them as functions defined on the set of real numbers

<!-- page 837 -->

by associating to any real number  $r$  the angle of  $r^\circ$ . In fact, we implicitly did this when we drew the graphs in Figures A2.5, A2.6, and A2.7.

However, just as there are a number of different measures of distance (inches, feet, yards, miles, centimeters, meters, etc.), there are a number of different measures of an angle. The most intuitive is the *degree*, which is defined by the fact that an angle of  $180^\circ$  yields a straight line and that an angle of  $360^\circ$  describes a complete revolution about the circle.

But why 360? Any number could have served about as well. Since the right angle is a natural cornerstone among angles, some engineers find it convenient to measure angles by what percentage of a right angle they are, using the term “grads” for the underlying unit. In this approach, a right angle is an angle of 100 grads, a  $45^\circ$  angle is an angle of 50 grads (50 percent of a right angle), and a  $60^\circ$  angle is an angle of  $66\frac{2}{3}$  grads ( $66\frac{2}{3}$  percent of a right angle).

In some sense, a more natural approach is to start with the unit circle — the circle of radius 1 pictured in Figure A2.3 — and to describe an angle by the *length of the arc* of the unit circle which that angle sweeps out. The word “radians” is used to describe this unit of measurement. Since the circumference of a circle of radius 1 is  $2\pi$ , an angle of  $360^\circ$  is the same as an angle of  $2\pi$  radians. A right angle, which corresponds to  $1/4$  of the way around the circle, is measured as

$$\frac{1}{4} \cdot 2\pi = \frac{\pi}{2} \text{ radians.}$$

In general, since  $360^\circ$  corresponds to  $2\pi$  radians, an angle of  $x^\circ$  is an angle of  $y$  radians where

$$\frac{1}{360}x = \frac{1}{2\pi}y,$$

so that

$$y = \frac{\pi}{180}x \quad \text{and} \quad x = \frac{180}{\pi}y.$$

The graph of the sine function in radians is presented in Figure A2.11.

![](pg_771__page_67_Figure_25.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A line graph showing the wave function y = sin(x) plotted on a Cartesian coordinate plane with x measured in radians from -2π to beyond 7π. The vertical y-axis ranges from -1 to +1, with tick marks at 1, 0, and -1. The horizontal x-axis shows labeled tick marks at intervals of π: -2π, -π, 0, π, 2π, 3π, 4π, 5π, 6π, and 7π. The sinusoidal curve starts at (0,0), rises to a peak of 1 near π/2, crosses the x-axis at π, drops to a minimum of -1 near 3π/2, and completes one full cycle of period 2π at 2π, repeating continuously across the domain.

The graph of  $x \mapsto \sin x$  with  $x$  measured in radians.

Figure  
A2.11

<!-- page 838 -->

If you are evaluating trig functions on a hand-held scientific calculator, you must tell the calculator the units with which you are measuring angles. When they are switched on, most scientific calculators start in degrees mode, and indicate so with DEG showing in a corner of the display window. To change units to radians or grads, push the DRG button on your calculator and watch the corresponding units change to RAD or GRAD in the window.

## A2.7 CALCULUS WITH TRIG FUNCTIONS

When one usexpresses calculus to work with trig functions, it is understood that all angles are measures in radians, because it is in radians that the derivatives have the simplest expressions. The key ingredient in the calculus approach to trig functions are the following two convergence results.

**Theorem A2.3** When angles  $x$  are measured in radians,

$$\frac{\sin x}{x} \rightarrow 1 \quad \text{as } x \rightarrow 0, \quad (9)$$

$$\frac{\cos x - 1}{x} \rightarrow 0 \quad \text{as } x \rightarrow 0. \quad (10)$$

The proof of these results takes us a little out of our way, and so it is presented at the end of this section. Figure A2.12 presents the intuition behind (9). Since angles are measured in radians, the size  $x$  of  $\angle BOC$  is the length of arc  $BD$  on the

![](pg_771__page_68_Picture_20.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A geometric diagram depicting a unit circle centered at the origin O on a Cartesian coordinate plane. A ray extends from the origin O into the first quadrant, passing through point B on the unit circle at a distance of 1 from the origin (radius OB = 1). From point B, a vertical line segment drops perpendicularly to the x-axis, meeting it at point C, forming a right-angled triangle OBC with a horizontal leg of length b_1 along the x-axis and a vertical leg BC of height b_2. The ray intersects the unit circle at point B, and point D is the intersection of the circle with the positive x-axis, showing arc BD.

**Figure  
A2.12**

*The geometry behind (9).*

<!-- page 839 -->

unit circle, and  $\sin x = \|BC\| = b_2$ . As  $x \rightarrow 0$ ,  $b_2 = \sin x \rightarrow 0$ , and the length of the  $arc$  BD becomes very close to the length of the *segment*  $\overline{BC}$ . (Use your calculator to see that (9) holds as  $x \rightarrow 0$  when  $x$  is measured in radians.)

Using the results of Theorem A2.3, one can easily compute the expressions for the derivatives of the six trigonometric functions.

**Theorem A2.4** Suppose that angles  $x$  are measures in radians. Then,

$$\begin{aligned} (\sin x)' &= \cos x, & (\cos x)' &= -\sin x, \\ (\tan x)' &= \sec^2 x, & (\cot x)' &= -\csc^2 x, \\ (\sec x)' &= \tan x \cdot \sec x, & (\csc x)' &= -\cot x \cdot \csc x. \end{aligned}$$

*Proof* The proof uses the definition of the derivative and the angle-sum formula in Theorem A2.2:

$$\begin{aligned} \frac{\sin(x+h) - \sin x}{h} &= \frac{\sin x \cos h + \cos x \sin h - \sin x}{h} \\ &= \sin x \left( \frac{\cos h - 1}{h} \right) + \cos x \left( \frac{\sin h}{h} \right). \end{aligned} \quad (11)$$

Now, let  $h$  tend to 0. By Theorem A2.3,  $(\cos h - 1)/h \rightarrow 0$  and  $(\sin h)/h \rightarrow 1$ . Therefore,

$$\begin{aligned} \frac{d}{dx} \sin x &\equiv \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{\sin(x+h) - \sin x}{h} \quad (\text{def. of derivative}) \\ &= \sin x \cdot 0 + \cos x \cdot 1 \quad (\text{by (11)}) \\ &= \cos x. \end{aligned}$$

Similarly,

$$\begin{aligned} \frac{\cos(x+h) - \cos x}{h} &= \frac{\cos x \cos h - \sin x \sin h - \cos x}{h} \\ &= \cos x \left( \frac{\cos h - 1}{h} \right) - \sin x \left( \frac{\sin h}{h} \right) \end{aligned}$$

tends to  $\cos x \cdot 0 - \sin x \cdot 1$  as  $h \rightarrow 0$ . Therefore,  $(\cos x)' = -\sin x$ . Since  $\tan$ ,  $\cot$ ,  $\sec$ , and  $\csc$  can be expressed as quotients of  $\sin$  and  $\cos$  (Theorem A2.1), their derivatives can now be calculated using the quotient rule of differentiation. (Exercise.) ■

<!-- page 840 -->

The Taylor series can be a powerful technique for working with nonlinear functions. As described in Section 30.3, the Taylor series of a function  $f: \mathbf{R} \rightarrow \mathbf{R}$  about the point  $x = 0$  is

$$f(0) + \frac{1}{1!}f'(0)x + \frac{1}{2!}f''(0)x^2 + \frac{1}{3!}f^{[3]}(0)x^3 + \frac{1}{4!}f^{[4]}(0)x^4 + \cdots. \quad (12)$$

Since  $(\sin x)' = \cos x$ ,  $(\cos x)' = -\sin x$ ,  $\sin 0 = 0$ , and  $\cos 0 = 1$ , the even order derivatives of the sine function are 0 at  $x = 0$ , while the odd order derivatives are  $\pm 1$ . The Taylor series for the sine function at  $x = 0$  is

$$x - \frac{1}{3!}x^3 + \frac{1}{5!}x^5 - \frac{1}{7!}x^7 + \frac{1}{9!}x^9 - \cdots. \quad (13)$$

Similarly, the Taylor series of the cosine function at  $x = 0$  is

$$1 - \frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \frac{1}{4!}x^4 - \frac{1}{6!}x^6 + \frac{1}{8!}x^8 + \cdots. \quad (14)$$

(Exercise.) For each fixed  $x$ , the consecutive terms of the series in (13) and (14) go to zero (rather rapidly). (Exercise.) It follows — with quite a bit of analysis — that the series in (13) and (14) converge *for any*  $x$ .

*Example A2.1* Taylor series yield an effective method for computing values of sin and cos. Let's use this method to compute  $\sin(\pi/4)$  to three decimal places. The first four terms in (13) for  $x = \pi/4$  are

$$\begin{aligned} x &\approx 0.7853982, & -\frac{x^3}{3!} &\approx -0.0807455, \\ \frac{x^5}{5!} &\approx 0.0024904, & \text{and} & -\frac{x^7}{7!} &\approx -0.0000366 \end{aligned} \quad (15)$$

Since further terms have zeros in the first five decimal places, they do not affect the first three digits of the Taylor series approximation of  $\sin(\pi/4)$ . Adding the four numbers in (15) yields

$$\sin \frac{\pi}{4} \approx 0.7071065,$$

an answer that is, in comparison with the true answer of  $1/\sqrt{2} \approx 0.7071068$  in (7), correct to six significant figures.

<!-- page 841 -->

**A2.9 PROOF OF THEOREM A2.3**

We close this section by sketching a proof of the basic convergence results in Theorem A2.3. Draw the unit circle  $S$ , as in Figure A2.13. Let  $\theta$  be an acute angle from the origin whose initial ray is the positive  $x$ -axis, as in Figures A2.3 and A2.13. Let  $B$  be the point where the terminal ray of  $\theta$  meets  $S$ . Let  $BC$  be the perpendicular line segment from the point  $B$  to the initial ray of  $\theta$ . Let  $A$  be the point  $(1, 0)$ , and let  $AD$  be the vertical line segment from  $A$  to the terminal ray of  $\theta$ .

![](pg_771__page_71_Picture_16.jpeg)

> **Image description:** Geometric diagram on a Cartesian coordinate plane featuring a unit circle centered at the origin O with radius 1. An acute angle theta is formed between the positive x-axis and a terminal ray passing through point B on the circle and extending to point D. Point A is at (1, 0) on the x-axis, which is the intersection of the unit circle and the positive x-axis. Segment BC is perpendicular to the x-axis from B to point C. Segment AD is perpendicular to the x-axis from A to point D on the terminal ray, forming right angles at C and A. Segments OB, AB, and line OD are drawn to illustrate the relationship between the areas of triangle OBA, sector OBA, and triangle ODA.

 $\triangle OBA \subset \text{sector } OBA \subset \triangle ODA.$ 

**Figure  
A2.13**

As Figure A2.13 indicates, triangle  $OBA \subset \text{sector } OBA \subset \text{triangle } ODA$ . Therefore,

$$\text{area of } \triangle OBA \leq \text{area of sector } OBA \leq \text{area of } \triangle ODA. \quad (16)$$

Now,

$$\text{area of } \triangle OBA = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \|OA\| \cdot \|BC\|$$

$$= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 1 \cdot \sin \theta,$$

$$\text{area of sector } OBA = \frac{\theta}{2\pi} \cdot \text{area inside } S$$

$$= \frac{\theta}{2\pi} \cdot \pi \cdot 1^2 = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \theta,$$

<!-- page 842 -->

$$\begin{aligned}\text{area of } \triangle ODA &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \|OA\| \cdot \|AD\| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 1 \cdot \tan \theta.\end{aligned}$$

Substituting these calculations into (16) yields

$$\frac{1}{2} \sin \theta \leq \frac{1}{2} \theta \leq \frac{1}{2} \tan \theta. \quad (17)$$

Divide (17) through by  $\frac{1}{2} \sin x$ :

$$1 \leq \frac{\theta}{\sin \theta} \leq \cos \theta,$$

and invert:

$$\frac{1}{\cos \theta} \leq \frac{\sin \theta}{\theta} \leq 1.$$

As  $\theta \rightarrow 0$ ,  $\cos \theta \rightarrow 1$  and  $(\sin \theta)/\theta$  is forced to converge to 1; this proves (9).

To prove (10), note that

$$\begin{aligned}\frac{\cos x - 1}{x} &= \frac{\cos x - 1}{x} \cdot \frac{\cos x + 1}{\cos x + 1} \\ &= \frac{\cos^2 x - 1}{x(\cos x + 1)} \\ &= \frac{-\sin^2 x}{x(\cos x + 1)} \quad (\text{by (4)}) \\ &= \frac{\sin x}{x} \cdot \frac{-\sin x}{\cos x + 1} \\ &\rightarrow 1 \cdot \frac{0}{2} = 0, \quad \text{as } x \rightarrow 0 \quad (\text{by (9)}).\end{aligned}$$

